+# Travel Visualiser — Routing & Map Enhancement
+
+You are a senior full-stack developer working on my existing GitHub project:
+
+https://github.com/dmpotekhin/Travel-Visualiser
+
+Your task is to **improve the existing application**, not rewrite it from scratch.
+
+## 1. First: analyse the existing project
+
+Before making any changes:
+
+1. Inspect the entire repository structure.
+2. Understand the current backend architecture.
+3. Understand the current frontend architecture.
+4. Identify how locations are geocoded.
+5. Identify how routes are currently calculated.
+6. Identify how GeoJSON is generated.
+7. Identify how MapLibre renders and animates routes.
+8. Identify the current HERE API integration.
+9. Identify configuration, environment variables and secrets handling.
+10. Run the existing tests and application if possible.
+
+Do not make architectural changes until you understand the current implementation.
+
+---
+
+# 2. Main goal
+
+Improve Travel Visualiser so that route calculation becomes a replaceable backend service.
+
+The application should support different routing providers without coupling the frontend to a particular provider.
+
+The desired architecture is:
+
+```text
+                    Travel Visualiser
+                           |
+                    +------v------+
+                    |   Routing   |
+                    |   Service   |
+                    +------+------+
+                           |
+             +-------------+-------------+
+             |             |             |
+             v             v             v
+          HERE           OSRM       GraphHopper
+             |             |             |
+             +-------------+-------------+
+                           |
+                           v
+                        GeoJSON
+                           |
+                           v
+                       MapLibre
+```
+
+The frontend should not know whether the route was generated by HERE, OSRM, GraphHopper or another provider.
+
+---
+
+# 3. Routing abstraction
+
+Introduce a routing abstraction appropriate for the existing programming language/framework.
+
+Conceptually:
+
+```text
+RoutingProvider
+    |
+    +-- HereRoutingProvider
+    |
+    +-- OsrmRoutingProvider
+    |
+    +-- GraphHopperRoutingProvider
+    |
+    +-- GreatCircleRoutingProvider
+```
+
+Do not blindly copy this structure if the existing project architecture suggests a better solution.
+
+The important requirement is:
+
+> Route calculation must depend on an abstraction rather than directly on a concrete external API.
+
+The abstraction should support at least:
+
+* origin
+* destination
+* transport type
+* distance
+* duration, if available
+* route geometry
+* provider information
+
+---
+
+# 4. Transport types
+
+The application should support different transport types.
+
+At minimum:
+
+```text
+CAR
+TRAIN
+PLANE
+WALK
+BICYCLE
+```
+
+Create an appropriate enum/model if the project does not already have one.
+
+The routing strategy should depend on transport type.
+
+For example:
+
+```text
+CAR      -> road routing provider
+WALK     -> pedestrian routing provider
+BICYCLE  -> bicycle routing provider
+PLANE    -> great-circle geometry / aviation data
+TRAIN    -> appropriate provider or fallback geometry
+```
+
+Do not pretend that a road-routing API provides an actual railway or flight route.
+
+For unsupported transport/provider combinations, implement a clear fallback strategy.
+
+---
+
+# 5. HERE integration
+
+The existing HERE integration should continue working.
+
+Do not remove it.
+
+Refactor it behind the new routing abstraction.
+
+The existing functionality should remain backward compatible.
+
+Move all provider-specific logic into the HERE implementation.
+
+Do not expose HERE-specific models directly to the rest of the application.
+
+---
+
+# 6. OSRM support
+
+Add OSRM as an optional routing provider.
+
+OSRM should primarily support:
+
+```text
+CAR
+```
+
+and, if supported by the configured OSRM profile:
+
+```text
+BICYCLE
+WALK
+```
+
+The provider must:
+
+1. Send the appropriate request to OSRM.
+2. Parse the response.
+3. Convert the returned geometry into the application's internal route model.
+4. Return a provider-independent route object.
+
+Do not make the frontend aware of OSRM response formats.
+
+The OSRM base URL must be configurable through environment/configuration.
+
+Do not hardcode API endpoints in business logic.
+
+---
+
+# 7. GraphHopper support
+
+Evaluate whether GraphHopper can be integrated cleanly with the existing project.
+
+If the integration is straightforward, implement it behind the same routing abstraction.
+
+If implementing GraphHopper would introduce unnecessary complexity, document the integration point and leave it as a future provider rather than adding unnecessary dependencies.
+
+Do not over-engineer the first implementation.
+
+---
+
+# 8. Fallback routing
+
+The application should remain usable when an external routing API is unavailable.
+
+Implement a fallback mechanism.
+
+For example:
+
+```text
+HERE
+  ↓
+if unavailable
+  ↓
+OSRM
+  ↓
+if unavailable
+  ↓
+Great-circle / Haversine fallback
+```
+
+The exact priority should be configurable.
+
+Do not silently hide provider failures.
+
+Log which provider was used and why a fallback was selected.
+
+---
+
+# 9. Configuration
+
+All external API configuration must be externalized.
+
+Examples:
+
+```text
+HERE_API_KEY
+OSRM_BASE_URL
+GRAPHHOPPER_API_KEY
+ROUTING_PROVIDER
+ROUTING_FALLBACK_ENABLED
+```
+
+Use the configuration mechanism already used by the project.
+
+Never commit API keys.
+
+Never place secrets in frontend source code.
+
+Update `.env.example` or equivalent configuration documentation.
+
+---
+
+# 10. GeoJSON
+
+The existing GeoJSON API should continue working.
+
+The frontend should receive provider-independent GeoJSON.
+
+For example:
+
+```json
+{
+  "type": "Feature",
+  "geometry": {
+    "type": "LineString",
+    "coordinates": []
+  },
+  "properties": {
+    "transport": "CAR",
+    "distanceKm": 123.4,
+    "durationMinutes": 120,
+    "provider": "HERE"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Adapt this structure to the existing application's actual API rather than unnecessarily breaking compatibility.
+
+The goal is to make route geometry independent from the routing provider.
+
+---
+
+# 11. MapLibre frontend
+
+Keep MapLibre.
+
+Do not replace MapLibre with Google Maps.
+
+The current visualization and animation functionality must continue working.
+
+Improve the frontend so that route segments can display different transport types.
+
+For example:
+
+```text
+Moscow
+   |
+   | 🚗
+   |
+St Petersburg
+   |
+   | 🚆
+   |
+Berlin
+   |
+   | ✈️
+   |
+Paris
+```
+
+Different transport types should be visually distinguishable.
+
+Possible implementation:
+
+```text
+CAR      -> solid line
+TRAIN    -> dashed line
+PLANE    -> curved line
+WALK     -> dotted line
+BICYCLE  -> appropriate route style
+```
+
+Use the existing design language where possible.
+
+Do not introduce unnecessary UI frameworks.
+
+---
+
+# 12. Route animation
+
+Preserve the current route animation.
+
+The animation should work with:
+
+* multiple route segments
+* different transport types
+* different geometry sources
+
+For example:
+
+```text
+Segment 1
+Moscow -> Berlin
+CAR
+
+Segment 2
+Berlin -> Paris
+TRAIN
+
+Segment 3
+Paris -> Madrid
+PLANE
+```
+
+The animation should transition between segments correctly.
+
+Avoid rewriting the animation engine unless necessary.
+
+---
+
+# 13. API design
+
+Review the existing API endpoints.
+
+Where appropriate, introduce an endpoint similar to:
+
+```text
+POST /api/routes
+```
+
+Example request:
+
+```json
+{
+  "segments": [
+    {
+      "from": "Moscow",
+      "to": "Berlin",
+      "transport": "CAR"
+    },
+    {
+      "from": "Berlin",
+      "to": "Paris",
+      "transport": "TRAIN"
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+The exact endpoint and JSON structure should follow the conventions already present in the project.
+
+Do not break existing API consumers without a strong reason.
+
+---
+
+# 14. Error handling
+
+Implement clear error handling for:
+
+* invalid locations
+* geocoding failures
+* routing API failures
+* timeout
+* rate limiting
+* unsupported transport
+* missing API key
+* invalid provider configuration
+
+External API errors must not crash the application.
+
+Return meaningful errors to the frontend.
+
+---
+
+# 15. Testing
+
+Add tests for the new functionality.
+
+At minimum cover:
+
+### Unit tests
+
+* routing provider selection
+* transport type selection
+* fallback logic
+* route conversion
+* GeoJSON generation
+* invalid provider configuration
+* external provider failures
+
+### Integration tests
+
+Test the routing API/service with mocked external providers.
+
+Do not make normal automated tests depend on live HERE/OSRM/GraphHopper services.
+
+Use mocks/stubs/test doubles.
+
+If the existing project has a testing framework, use it instead of introducing another one.
+
+---
+
+# 16. Architecture quality
+
+Follow these principles:
+
+* SOLID
+* separation of concerns
+* dependency inversion
+* configuration over hardcoding
+* provider-independent domain models
+* clear error handling
+* testability
+* minimal duplication
+
+Avoid over-engineering.
+
+Do not introduce abstractions that are not needed.
+
+Prefer small, understandable classes/modules.
+
+---
+
+# 17. Documentation
+
+Update the README with:
+
+1. New architecture.
+2. Supported routing providers.
+3. Supported transport types.
+4. Environment variables.
+5. How to run the application.
+6. How to configure HERE.
+7. How to configure OSRM.
+8. Fallback behaviour.
+9. API examples.
+10. Example screenshots if appropriate.
+
+Add a simple architecture diagram using Mermaid if the repository already supports Markdown diagrams.
+
+Example:
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+    Frontend[MapLibre Frontend]
+    API[Route API]
+    Routing[Routing Service]
+    HERE[HERE Provider]
+    OSRM[OSRM Provider]
+    Fallback[Fallback Provider]
+
+    Frontend --> API
+    API --> Routing
+    Routing --> HERE
+    Routing --> OSRM
+    Routing --> Fallback
+```
+
+---
+
+# 18. Important constraints
+
+DO NOT:
+
+* rewrite the entire project
+* replace MapLibre with Google Maps
+* remove the existing HERE integration
+* hardcode API keys
+* put API keys into frontend JavaScript
+* make frontend code dependent on HERE/OSRM response formats
+* make automated tests call live external APIs
+* introduce unnecessary dependencies
+* break existing functionality without documenting the reason
+
+Before changing existing functionality, understand how it works.
+
+---
+
+# 19. Development process
+
+Work incrementally.
+
+### Phase 1
+
+Analyse the repository and existing architecture.
+
+### Phase 2
+
+Create the routing abstraction.
+
+### Phase 3
+
+Move the existing HERE implementation behind the abstraction.
+
+### Phase 4
+
+Implement OSRM.
+
+### Phase 5
+
+Implement fallback routing.
+
+### Phase 6
+
+Update GeoJSON/API.
+
+### Phase 7
+
+Update MapLibre visualization for transport types.
+
+### Phase 8
+
+Update animation.
+
+### Phase 9
+
+Add tests.
+
+### Phase 10
+
+Update documentation.
+
+After each significant phase:
+
+* run tests
+* verify the application
+* check for regressions
+* keep the changes focused
+
+---
+
+# 20. Final deliverable
+
+At the end provide:
+
+1. Summary of implemented changes.
+2. Architecture changes.
+3. List of modified/created files.
+4. New environment variables.
+5. API changes.
+6. Routing providers implemented.
+7. Transport types supported.
+8. Tests added.
+9. Known limitations.
+10. Instructions for running the application.
+11. Suggestions for the next iteration.
+
+Most importantly:
+
+**Work with the existing Travel Visualiser architecture. Extend it rather than replacing it.**
